// `src/core/vec.npk` — `Vec<T>`, THE GROWABLE ARRAY, AND THE ONLY BOUNDS CHECK
// THIS LIBRARY HAS.
//
// Rule B-11 (RX-006) declares it ours rather than imported: the shape is the
// compiler's `List<T>` — a `wild` block, a count, a capacity, doubling through
// `#size_of<T>()` — because that shape is right and has been exercised across
// twenty-two families, and ours because a library must not import a compiler's
// internals. RX-126 is why the resemblance stops there: the compiler's D-247
// made the compiler-known `List<T>` OWNING, and `decl_is_list` keys that on a
// file named `list`, a struct named `List`, and exactly the fields
// `items`/`count`/`cap`. This is `Vec` in `vec.npk`, so it is an ordinary
// struct holding a bare pointer, it does not drop, and every consequence below
// follows from that.
//
// ============================================================================
// THE ONE THING TO READ BEFORE CHANGING ANYTHING HERE
// ============================================================================
//
// **`items` IS A `wild T->`, AND INDEXING IT IS NOT BOUNDS-CHECKED.**
// `SAFETY.md` §5.3, rule S-23 (RX-111). D-070's guarantee attaches to types
// that CARRY A LENGTH — a slice `T[]` and a fixed array `T[N]` trap on an
// out-of-range index. A `wild T->` is a bare pointer and the emitter goes
// straight to `getelementptr` with no guard. Measured as a pair at the same
// offset and the same program shape: `probe08c` (a slice, index 999 into four
// elements) exits **94**, `OutOfBounds`; `probe08b` (a `wild int64->` block,
// the same index) exits **0**, having read 7 992 bytes past its allocation and
// returned the result.
//
// So an out-of-range index here is **a WRONG ANSWER, not a crash** — which
// inverts the failure mode this library advertises. A wrong program counter
// reads an unrelated heap word as an instruction; a wrong sparse-set probe adds
// a thread for a state the automaton is not in and the library returns a match
// that is not there. Both silent, both reachable from bytes a caller does not
// control.
//
// THEREFORE:
//
//   1. `vec_get` and `vec_set` are the ONLY sanctioned way to read or write an
//      element, and they check. They are not tidiness; since RX-111 they are
//      the library's entire bounds check.
//   2. **NO `.items[` MAY APPEAR OUTSIDE THIS FILE.** A tree check enforces it.
//      Inside this file it appears only below a check that has already run.
//      *(Since cycle 0.0.4c THE COMPILER ENFORCES IT TOO, for every module but
//      `vec`: `items` is `hidden`, so any `.items` outside this file is
//      NITPICK-TYPE-080 -- RX-153. The tree check stays, because it also
//      watches THIS file -- an owner that no longer uses its own accessor
//      fails -- and it watches `.ptr[`, which sealing `Bytes.buf` does not
//      hide.)*
//   3. **SIGNEDNESS IS HALF THE CHECK.** An index derived from an `int32` field
//      can be NEGATIVE, `i < count` accepts it, and a negative index reads
//      backwards off the block without complaint. Every accessor checks
//      `0 <= i` as well as `i < count`. This is the half a reader porting from
//      C leaves out, because there the index is `unsigned`.
//   4. **THE ELEMENT OWNS NOTHING** (`SAFETY.md` S-23a, RX-155). `Vec<T>` is for
//      a non-owning `T`, and NOTHING IN THE LANGUAGE SAYS SO -- the section
//      "WHAT A `Vec` MAY HOLD" below has the verbs, one by one.
//      *(Since cycle 0.0.4e THE COMPILER SAYS SO FOR `vec_get`, the one verb
//      that hands an element back: it takes `T: Pod`, and an owning type
//      cannot implement `Pod` as it is declared -- RX-168.)*
//   5. **A `Vec` IS MOVE-ONLY, AND THE COMPILER SAYS SO** (`SAFETY.md` S-23b,
//      RX-161, since cycle 0.0.4d). A copy of one -- or of any struct holding
//      one -- is NITPICK-TYPE-046. A BY-VALUE PARAMETER IS NOT A COPY, IT IS A
//      LOAN, and it still reaches the block: the section "MOVE-ONLY, AND WHAT A
//      LOAN STILL REACHES" below says how far (RX-162).
//      *(Since cycle 0.0.4e, compiler `c970483`, the compiler holds the loan
//      READ-ONLY: `@v` of a lent `Vec` is NITPICK-TYPE-085 -- RX-169.)*
//
// ============================================================================
// WHAT A VIOLATION DOES, AND WHY IT IS A TRAP (RX-130)
// ============================================================================
//
// It traps `OutOfBounds`, exit 94 in this repository's `failsafe` convention.
// Not a `Result`: `SAFETY.md` S-4 (RX-061) says matching cannot fail and
// `regex_find` returns `Match?` and not `Result<Match?>`, so an accessor with
// an error channel would put one on the search path — the hottest path in the
// library — and every engine would have to thread it. And a trap is where the
// language is going anyway: when the compiler's 1.5.3 lands `requires`, a
// contract violation takes the TRAP route (its D-241), so writing the trap now
// means the behaviour does not change on the day the clause is uncommented.
// *(Dated 2026-09-25, cycle 0.0.4b, compiler `c3bdae2`: 1.5.3 has landed and the
// trap route is right -- `probe13g` measures it -- but the last clause is not.
// With `vec_get`'s obligation made live, the read of an empty `Vec` traps
// `RequiresViolated` (116), NOT `OutOfBounds` (94), and every consumer owes the
// new arm: uncommenting the clause WOULD change the behaviour, by changing the
// trap's identity. So the obligations below stay comments, by
// `meta/OPEN_QUESTIONS.md` Q-6, which carries this cost -- RX-152.)*
// *(Dated 2026-09-25, cycle 0.0.4d: Q-6 is answered -- A′, RX-164,
// `VERIFICATION.md` P-1b. An obligation stays a comment unless a numbered
// decision accepts the arm its live clause adds to every consumer, and none is
// taken here, so this stop keeps its identity, 94.)*
//
// The trap is spelled by indexing a one-element fixed array out of range —
// `int64[1]`, which DOES carry a length — so it is the language's own
// `OutOfBounds` and not a code this library invented. It costs nothing on the
// fast path: the array is constructed inside the failing branch, which does not
// return.
//
// ============================================================================
// A `Vec` WITH `cap <= 0` IS DEAD, AND EVERY GROWTH PATH TRAPS ON ONE (RX-139)
// ============================================================================
//
// `vec_free` sets `count = 0; cap = 0` DELIBERATELY, as poison: it is what makes
// `vec_get`, `vec_set` and `vec_pop` trap on a freed `Vec` instead of reading
// through a dangling `items`. The poisoning was right and INCOMPLETE — the three
// growth paths read `cap` as an arithmetic starting point and none of them
// checked it. Measured at `3d15ac9` on a `Vec` that had been `vec_free`d:
//
//   * `vec_reserve(@v, 1)`  --  `nc = v.cap` is 0 and `while (nc < want)
//     { nc = nc * 2 }` multiplies zero forever. **exit 124 under `timeout 6`:
//     IT DOES NOT TERMINATE.** That is a denial of service in the container
//     every engine in this library is built on, reached with no backtracking at
//     all — against the first of `CLAUDE.md`'s non-negotiable rules.
//   * `vec_push` / `vec_insert`  --  `nc = v.cap * 2` is 0, so they call
//     `ralloc(<dangling>, 0)`. **exit 91, `HeapBadRequest`**, because the
//     runtime refuses `ralloc(p, 0)` outright (D-150: freeing is spelled
//     `dalloc`, and C's `realloc(p, 0)` is the implementation-defined footgun
//     it is not). So those two already stop — but they stop on the RUNTIME's
//     check of a size, not on this library's check of its own invariant, and
//     they report the wrong thing: `HeapBadRequest` says the allocator was
//     misused, when what happened is that a freed container was written to.
//
// **THE FIX IS A TRAP, NOT A FLOOR, AND THE SIBLING IS NOT THE PRECEDENT.**
// `bytes_reserve` carries `if (nc < 1i64) { nc = 1i64; }` and the obvious move
// was to copy it here. It is the wrong move: `Bytes` has no free, its `buf` is
// MANAGED, and `bytes_reserve` allocates a fresh `buffer_new` — so flooring a
// zero capacity there produces a valid buffer. Here the block is already
// `dalloc`ed, so flooring `nc` to 1 would hand `ralloc` a DANGLING pointer with
// a plausible size and let it succeed. That trades a loud hang for silent heap
// corruption, which is the wrong direction for this library in every case.
// The same guard in two files would have been two different decisions.
//
// So `cap <= 0` traps `OutOfBounds` through `vec_oob`, like every other misuse
// in this file, and it traps at the TOP of the entry point rather than inside
// the growth branch: `vec_reserve(@v, 0)` on a freed `Vec` would otherwise
// return `NIL` and report success about a container that no longer exists.
// A `Vec` this library produced can never reach the check — `vec_init` and
// `vec_init_zeroed` both floor the block to one element — so it costs one
// perfectly-predicted compare and fires only on a use-after-free.
//
// **"LIKE EVERY OTHER MISUSE IN THIS FILE" WAS NOT TRUE OF THE FREE PATHS, AND
// NOW IS — RX-144.** RX-139 put the guard on the three GROWTH paths and left
// the two DESTRUCTION paths alone, so a double `vec_free`, a `vec_free_owning`
// after a `vec_free`, and a double `sset_free` all exited **95, `Unreachable`**
// — measured at `3d15ac9`. They stopped, deterministically, on the ALLOCATOR's
// double-free check. That is precisely the complaint this header makes six
// paragraphs above about `vec_push` and `vec_insert` — *they stop on the
// runtime's check of a size, not on this library's check of its own invariant,
// and they report the wrong thing* — fixed there and left standing here, in the
// same header that states it. **A sentence that says "like every other X"
// is a claim about a set, and the set was never enumerated.** The same
// `cap <= 0` guard now heads `vec_free` and `vec_free_owning`, and `sset_free`
// inherits it by calling them; a double free is `OutOfBounds` at this library's
// own check, not `Unreachable` from inside `dalloc`.
//
// *(Dated 2026-09-25, the third cycle-0.0 audit's N-14 and N-15 -- RX-156. TWO
// THINGS IN THAT PARAGRAPH WERE WRONG. (1) "They report the wrong thing" is
// true of `vec_push` and `vec_insert`, whose 91 said the allocator was misused,
// and FALSE of the free paths: the runtime's trap table, read at `c3bdae2`,
// files a double free under `-4102 HEAP_INTEGRITY`, and the prelude's
// `Unreachable = 4102i32` is "`#unreachable()` reached, and the runtime's
// integrity defects". So 95 was the language's own code for exactly this, from
// the allocator's check. The guard's 94 is a TRADE, and it stays: the stop is
// earlier, it is this library's, it names the value, and every use of a dead
// `Vec` stops the same way -- at the price that a consumer's `failsafe` can no
// longer tell a double free from an out-of-range index. (2) "A double free is
// `OutOfBounds`" holds for the SAME binding only. The guard reads the `cap` of
// the header it is handed, and a whole-`Vec` copy keeps the old one:
// `Vec<int64>:w = v;` then `vec_free(@v)` and `vec_free(@w)` reaches `dalloc`
// and exits 95, and `vec_free_owning` through such a copy walks the freed block
// first and exits 95 too (both measured at `c3bdae2`, -O0 and `opt -O2`). That
// is N-15, OPEN against the board's question 9 with the rest of what a header
// copy reaches -- see the paragraph above `struct:Vec`.)*
//
// *(Dated 2026-09-25, the fourth cycle-0.0 audit's BL-8 -- RX-160. N-15 was
// deferred on a false premise -- that nothing copies a `Vec` before cycle 0.8 --
// and its reach is wider than one binding: a whole-`SparseSet` copy freed twice
// exits 95 too, and read after the free reports a member ABSENT while its count
// says one, with no trap. N-15 is DEFERRED TO 0.0.4d, BEFORE CYCLE 0.0 CLOSES,
// by the author's decision on the board's question 9: `Vec` becomes MOVE-ONLY
// BY CONSTRUCTION, after which the second handle this guard cannot see does
// not compile. `tests/unit/vec_alias_*.npk` and `sparseset_alias_*.npk` pin
// today's behaviour and are what 0.0.4d turns into rejection fixtures.)*
//
// ============================================================================
// MOVE-ONLY, AND WHAT A LOAN STILL REACHES (S-23b; RX-161, RX-162)
// ============================================================================
//
// **SINCE CYCLE 0.0.4d A `Vec` CANNOT BE COPIED.** The struct's last field is
// `hidden string[0]:move_only` -- a fixed array of NO strings. It holds nothing
// and occupies nothing (`#size_of<Vec<int64>>()` is 24, as it was), and because
// its element type owns, the compiler's layout walk marks every `Vec<T>` as
// owning (`type_drops`, D-183) -- and an owning type is move-only. So
// `Vec<int64>:w = v;` is NITPICK-TYPE-046, and so is a copy of anything that
// holds a `Vec`: `SparseSet:t = s;`, `COMPILE.md` C-1's `Program`, the parser's
// state. A transfer is `move(v)`, which invalidates `v` (D-065). The three
// shapes the fourth cycle-0.0 audit measured through a copy -- a double free
// (95), a read of the free poison, a `SparseSet` member reading ABSENT -- no
// longer compile (`tests/rejection/vec_alias_*.npk`, `sparseset_alias_*.npk`).
// Measured at `c3bdae2`: the drop the compiler now generates for a `Vec` walks
// the empty array and frees NOTHING -- the block stays `wild`, freed only by
// `vec_free`, and a `Vec` never freed still traps `WildLeak` at `exit 0` -- and
// no consumer's `failsafe` bill moves. A marker whose element owns nothing,
// `int64[0]`, changes nothing at all: the element's ownership is the mechanism.
//
// **A BY-VALUE PARAMETER IS A LOAN, NOT A COPY, AND IT STILL REACHES THE
// BLOCK.** An ordinary parameter is one the callee may read and may not keep
// (the compiler's D-065 and D-183): a move-only `Vec` is passed to one WITHOUT
// `move`, and `move(v)` inside the callee is NITPICK-TYPE-047. `vec_get` below
// is such a reader and keeps its signature. But the callee may take its own
// parameter's address, `@v`, and free or grow through it, and the caller's
// header then names a block the callee released: a read of the free poison, a
// double free (95), a `SparseSet` member ABSENT with no trap
// (`tests/unit/vec_alias_param_*.npk`, `sparseset_alias_param_free.npk`). For
// an owning FIELD the compiler goes further: overwriting one through a lent
// parameter DROPS THE CALLER'S VALUE -- a double free with no `wild` block in
// the program (`tests/probe/probe17_lent_field_drop.npk`), which is how a lent
// `Bytes` grown by its callee dies (`bytes_alias_param_grow.npk`). No type here
// can refuse a loan. That is a compiler defect, raised and not worked around
// (RX-162); until it is fixed, a callee that must change a `Vec` takes
// `Vec<T>->`, as every function below does.
//
// *(Dated 2026-09-25, the fifth cycle-0.0 audit's N-25 and N-26 -- RX-167. "A
// `Vec` CANNOT BE COPIED" is true of a COPY, which is NITPICK-TYPE-046, and not of
// every second handle. A GENERIC function that passes out its lent `T` --
// `func:id<T> = T(T:x) { pass x; }` -- compiles at `T = Vec<int64>` and hands back
// a second owner of the block, because TYPE-047 is not asked of a lent `T` in a
// generic body (the workbench registry's O-N22, the compiler's DEF-104, riding
// with its 1.6.0 step 3g; `tests/unit/vec_alias_generic_passout.npk`). And a `for`
// binding over an array of `Vec`s is a loan like a parameter, through which a loop
// frees each element's block (`tests/unit/vec_alias_for_binding_free.npk`). No
// function below has either shape: no generic here takes a lent bare `T`, and
// there is no `for`. Both are pinned, not endorsed, and the re-pin to a compiler
// carrying 3g measures whether each is refused.)*
//
// *(Dated 2026-09-26, cycle 0.0.4e, compiler `c970483` -- RX-169. The re-pin
// measured each face, and all are REFUSED: a write through a lent container --
// `@v` of a parameter, `@x` of a `for` binding, an owning field overwritten --
// is NITPICK-TYPE-085 (the compiler's DEF-102), and the generic pass-out is
// NITPICK-TYPE-047 (DEF-104). The six pins and probe 17 are refusals now, each
// refused exactly its code at the `@` or the `pass`, and each still RUNS at
// `c3bdae2`, so each refusal is the pin's (`tests/rejection/vec_alias_param_*`,
// `sparseset_alias_param_free`, `bytes_alias_param_grow`,
// `vec_alias_for_binding_free`, `vec_alias_generic_passout`;
// `tests/probe/refused/probe17_lent_field_drop`). The spellings the refusals
// prescribe run clean (`tests/unit/loan_spellings.npk`): a callee that frees
// takes `move Vec<T>:v` and its caller writes `move(v)`, one that grows takes
// `Vec<T>->`. "No generic here takes a lent bare `T`" stays true and was not
// the whole of DEF-104's reach: it refuses a `T` PLACE passed out of a lent or
// pointed-to container too, which was `vec_get` and `vec_pop` -- see each
// (RX-168).)*
//
// ============================================================================
// LIFETIME, AND WHAT `exit 0` PROVES ABOUT IT (P-20, S-22)
// ============================================================================
//
// A `Vec`'s block is `wild`, and its lifetime is its owner's scope: every scope
// that makes one calls `vec_free`, on every path, or `exit 0` traps `WildLeak`
// under D-151.
//
// **THAT COVERS THE BLOCK AND STOPS THERE.** D-151 counts `wild` blocks, D-188
// counts live drivers, and NEITHER SEES A MANAGED BODY (`SAFETY.md` §8b, S-22).
// So `vec_free` on a `Vec` whose elements own heap storage releases the block
// and orphans the elements, at exit 0, silently — `nitpick-time` measured
// exactly this shape retaining 125 MiB over two million elements. Where the
// obligation is managed the gate is a MEMORY CAP, never an exit code, and
// `vec_free_owning` below is the function that discharges it.
//
// ============================================================================
// WHAT A `Vec` MAY HOLD: A `T` THAT OWNS NOTHING (S-23a, RX-155)
// ============================================================================
//
// **NOTHING IN THE LANGUAGE KEEPS AN OWNER OUT OF THIS TYPE, AND THIS FILE SAID
// FOR A CYCLE THAT SOMETHING DID.** `TYPE-046` is the diagnostic that asks for
// `move` when an owning PLACE is copied; `pass` moves implicitly, and D-264
// checks every generic body here once with `T` treated as owning -- so what
// compiles at any `T` compiles at every `T`, `Vec<string>` included. The third
// cycle-0.0 audit (BL-5) measured what each verb then does, and it is re-measured
// at `c3bdae2` by one unit per verb, so the table is a run and not a belief:
//
//   ownership-correct   `vec_push`, `vec_insert`, `vec_pop`, `vec_free_owning`
//   MOVES THE ELEMENT   `vec_get` -- the slot is emptied and still counted
//   ORPHANS             `vec_set` (what it overwrites), `vec_remove`,
//                       `vec_swap_remove` (what they remove), `vec_truncate`,
//                       `vec_clear` (what they discard), `vec_free` (all of it)
//
// So `Vec<T>` is for a `T` that DROPS NOTHING AND HOLDS NO BLOCK OF ITS OWN, and
// at such a `T` every verb is correct and a move is a copy. Every `Vec` the
// specification declares holds one -- `Inst`, `ByteSet`, `uint8`, `HirNode`,
// `ClassRange`, and the engines' `int32` and `int64` -- or a type it has not yet
// shaped, `Literal`, `GroupInfo` and the parser's `Frame`, which S-23a requires
// to be shaped the way H-2 shapes group names: offsets into a `Bytes`. **`check_vec_elements_own_nothing` enforces it over `src/`**, default-deny since RX-158 -- it clears only what it can see owns nothing -- which is
// the code that ships; `tests/` instantiates `Vec<string>` on purpose, to
// measure the table above. A cycle that needs an owning element lifts S-23a by a
// decision, and the compiler's own `List<T>` is the shape it would take:
// removals that return the element, discards that drop it, and no by-value get.
// *(Dated 2026-09-26, cycle 0.0.4e, compiler `c970483` -- RX-168. The table's
// second row is a COMPILE ERROR now. The pin refuses `vec_get` as it was written
// at every `T` -- a generic body is checked once with `T` owning (D-264), and
// since DEF-104 that is asked of a `T` place passed out of a loan too -- so
// `vec_get` takes `T: Pod`, and at `Vec<string>` it is NITPICK-TYPE-017
// (`tests/rejection/vec_owning_get_moves_out.npk`). The other rows are unchanged,
// and S-23a and its check stand for them.)*
//
// LAYERING (`BUILD.md` §6, B-16). `core` imports nothing outside `core`. This
// file imports nothing at all.
mod:vec;

// ---- the type --------------------------------------------------------------

// THE FIELDS SAY WHO MAY TOUCH THEM, SINCE CYCLE 0.0.4c (RX-153; the
// compiler's D-313, D-314 and D-308). `items` is `hidden`: outside this module
// it is neither read nor written, NITPICK-TYPE-080. `count` and `cap` are
// `sealed`: read anywhere, written only here, NITPICK-TYPE-079 -- and a
// consumer's struct literal names all three, so it is refused for each. Both
// counts carry the prelude's `ListLen`, `{ $ >= 0, $ <= 2^47 }`: checked after
// EVERY write in EVERY build, trapping `LimitViolated`, so a count driven
// negative traps at the write that drove it (BL-3 re-planted at `c3bdae2`:
// 109 there, where the unsealed tree ran on to exit 60). The verified build's
// payoff -- the counts' arithmetic rows becoming dischargeable, because every
// read of a limited field is a fact -- is the board's forecast, not something
// a plain build does.
//
// UNTIL 0.0.4c THIS PARAGRAPH SAID THE FIELDS WERE PUBLIC "BECAUSE THE
// LANGUAGE HAS NO OTHER KIND", which was true at every pin this repository took
// before `c3bdae2`: a consumer's literal compiled, linked and ran. WHAT THE
// QUALIFIERS DO NOT CLOSE, measured at `c3bdae2`: `wild` storage reinterpreted
// by `=>!` -- a forged `Vec` reads `count == -1` -- which is the language's
// opt-out for every checked property; and a WHOLE-`Vec` COPY, `Vec<int64>:w =
// v;`, which names no field and is a second handle on the block: after
// `vec_free(@v)`, `vec_get(w, 0)` reads the free poison (exit 170) and
// `vec_free(@w)` is a double free (95, the third audit's N-15) -- as is
// `vec_free_owning` through a copy, which first walks the freed block (95).
// `meta/roadmap/done/0.0/0.0.4c.md` records both; the board's question 9 carries
// them to the author.
// *(Dated 2026-09-25 -- RX-160. The copy is not the only second handle: a
// BY-VALUE `Vec<T>` parameter is one, and a callee may free its caller's block
// through it (`tests/unit/vec_alias_param_free.npk`); and any struct holding a
// `Vec` copies it silently (`vec_alias_struct_copy.npk`). `vec_get` below takes
// its `Vec` by value and is harmless only because it never frees -- so the
// author's answer, MOVE-ONLY BY CONSTRUCTION at 0.0.4d before cycle 0.0
// closes, changes its signature and every by-value read in `src/core/`.)*
// *(Dated 2026-09-25, cycle 0.0.4d -- RX-161, RX-162. The copy is closed by the
// last field below; the header's "MOVE-ONLY, AND WHAT A LOAN STILL REACHES"
// says how. The last clause above is FALSE, measured at `c3bdae2`: a by-value
// parameter is a LOAN, which compiles for an owning type without `move`, so
// `vec_get` and every by-value read in `src/core/` are unchanged. What a loan
// reaches is not closed by any type, and the header says whose it is.)*
// *(Dated 2026-09-26, cycle 0.0.4e, compiler `c970483` -- RX-168, RX-169.
// `vec_get` keeps its by-value `Vec<T>:v` and gains a bound, `T: Pod`, because
// the pin refuses its old body; and a loan IS closed now -- by the compiler,
// which refuses every write through one, NITPICK-TYPE-085.)*
pub struct:Vec<T> = {
    hidden wild T->:items;                 // WILD, DELIBERATELY. See the header.
    sealed limit<ListLen> int64:count;     // elements in use
    sealed limit<ListLen> int64:cap;       // elements the block can hold
    hidden string[0]:move_only;            // ZERO BYTES, HOLDS NOTHING -- AND MAKES `Vec` MOVE-ONLY (RX-161)
};

// ---- the trap --------------------------------------------------------------

// THE OUT-OF-RANGE STOP, FOR THE WHOLE `core` LAYER. **IT DOES NOT RETURN, FOR
// ANY `i`** — and the first spelling of it did return, for exactly one.
//
// **RX-143, AND IT IS THE LARGEST DEFECT THIS CYCLE FOUND.** The body used to
// be `discard(guard[i]);`, which is out of range for every `i` EXCEPT 0 —
// and 0 is precisely the index that nine `i >= <count|len>` guards in this
// layer pass when the container is EMPTY. Every call site is `drop vec_oob(…)`
// and `drop` CONTINUES, so for those nine the guard branch was taken, the stop
// did not stop, and the accessor performed the very read or write it had just
// refused. Measured at `3d15ac9`: `vec_get` on an empty `Vec` returned a heap
// word; `vec_set` on a FREED `Vec` completed the write through a dangling
// `items`; `vec_remove` left `count == -1`, after which `vec_push` wrote at
// `items[-1]`, before the block, and the damage surfaced later as a corrupted
// heap header; `sset_at` returned a phantom member — verbatim the failure
// `sparseset.npk`'s own header warns of.
//
// **THE INDEX IS NEGATIVE FOR EVERY `i`, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF THE FIX**, and
// negative rather than merely-not-zero on purpose: a negative index is out of
// range for an array of ANY length, so the trap does not depend on the guard
// array one line below it staying one element long. `i & 1i64` is 0 or 1, so
// the index is −1 or −2 — never in range, for this array or for any array, at
// any `i` including `int64`'s minimum and maximum.
//
// `i` IS STILL A PARAMETER, AND THE REASON PRINTED HERE BEFORE WAS WRONG.
// It said `i` was passed "only so the trap is reached with a value the
// optimiser cannot prove constant". **Measured at `3d15ac9` and it is false:**
// a literal out-of-range index compiles at exit 0, `npkc` folds it to an
// unconditional `call void @npk_trap(i32 -4099)`, and the program exits 94 at
// −O0 and through `opt -O2` alike. Folding does not defeat this trap at this
// pin. The reasons the parameter survives are the two that are true — the call
// site says WHICH index was out of range, which is the whole of the diagnosis a
// reader gets; and a compiler that later refuses a constant out-of-range index
// outright would stop a parameterless spelling from compiling at all.
//
// `pub`, and called from `bytes.npk` as well, because ONE definition of the
// stop is worth an odd-looking prefix: two copies would be two places for the
// trap to weaken, which is the argument RX-123 makes about a rule restated
// rather than cited. B-14 gives it the declaring module's prefix, so it stays
// `vec_oob` at every call site. **That argument is now paid for twice over:
// one definition meant one line to fix, and nine broken call sites needed no
// edit at all.**
//
// obligation, in the syntax it takes since the compiler's 1.5.3 (a comment,
// by A′ -- `VERIFICATION.md` P-1b, RX-164, the answer to
// `meta/OPEN_QUESTIONS.md` Q-6):
//     never fails ensures false
// — it is `never fails` and it carries a contract, which is legal since the
// compiler's D-241 retired D-163 rule 2's row (RX-127). This library's own
// cycle 0.0.0 record says the pair is a permanent `NITPICK-TYPE-037` refusal;
// that was true at pin `950bb1d` and is false now. **`ensures false` was the
// stated obligation while the body returned for `i == 0`, so the contract was
// false at the one value that mattered and no instrument in this repository
// could see it — the clause was a comment the compiler would have refused,
// and at `c3bdae2`, where it would be live, it is a comment by A′ (RX-164).**
//
// GUARDED BY `vec_oob_selfcheck.npk`, which asserts the stop for
// k ∈ {−1, 0, 1} and is the one unit that would have caught this. Twelve
// out-of-range units existed when the defect shipped and all twelve passed a
// non-zero argument.
pub func:vec_oob = NIL(int64:i) never fails {
    // `int64[1]` CARRIES A LENGTH, so D-070's guard is emitted for it and the
    // index traps. This is the language's `OutOfBounds`, not an invented code.
    int64[1]:guard = [0i64];
    // −1 or −2. Out of range for every `i`, and out of range for any length
    // this array could later be given. See the header for why not `guard[i]`.
    int64:below = 0i64 - 1i64 - (i & 1i64);
    discard(guard[below]);
    pass NIL;
};

// ---- construction and destruction ------------------------------------------

// obligation: never fails ensures result.count == 0i64 && result.cap >= 1i64
//
// A capacity of at least one, always, so `vec_push`'s doubling has something to
// double and no path multiplies zero by two forever.
pub func:vec_init<T> = Vec<T>(int64:cap) never fails {
    int64:n = cap;
    if (n < 1i64) { n = 1i64; }
    // `n * #size_of<T>()` traps on overflow (D-210), and that trap is the
    // guard: a request this large cannot be satisfied, so a controlled stop is
    // the right answer and no invented bound is needed. `alloc` itself traps on
    // OOM rather than returning null.
    wild int8->:mem = alloc(n * #size_of<T>());
    pass Vec{ items: mem =>! wild T->, count: 0i64, cap: n, move_only: [] };
};

// obligation: never fails ensures v.cap == 0i64
//
// FREES THE BLOCK AND NOTHING ELSE. For an owning `T` this is a leak that
// `exit 0` cannot see — use `vec_free_owning`. The name does not say "owning"
// because the only `T` this library may hold is one that owns nothing
// (`SAFETY.md` S-23a, RX-155), and a name that warned on every call would stop
// being read. *(It said until the third cycle-0.0 audit's triage that "TYPE-046
// forces it for anything stored in an array". Nothing forces it: an owning
// element compiles, links and runs in a `Vec` and in a fixed array alike --
// measured at `950bb1d`, `3d15ac9` and `c3bdae2`. The POD shape is this
// library's design, and S-23a is what now makes it a rule.)*
pub func:vec_free<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v) never fails {
    // DEAD `Vec` -- see the header. Without this, a double free reaches
    // `dalloc` with a pointer the allocator has already reclaimed and stops on
    // ITS check, reporting `Unreachable` about a heap invariant instead of
    // `OutOfBounds` about this library's. RX-144.
    if (v.cap <= 0i64) { drop vec_oob(v.cap - 1i64); }
    dalloc(v.items =>! wild int8->);
    v.count = 0i64;
    v.cap = 0i64;
    pass NIL;
};

// ---- capacity --------------------------------------------------------------

// obligation: never fails ensures v.cap >= v.count + need
pub func:vec_reserve<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:need) never fails {
    if (need < 0i64)  { drop vec_oob(need); }
    // DEAD `Vec` -- see the header. Without this the loop below multiplies zero
    // by two forever: measured exit 124 under `timeout 6`.
    if (v.cap <= 0i64) { drop vec_oob(v.cap - 1i64); }
    int64:want = v.count + need;              // traps on overflow (D-210)
    if (want <= v.cap) { pass NIL; }
    int64:nc = v.cap;
    while (nc < want) decreases want - nc { nc = nc * 2i64; }     // traps on overflow (D-210)
    wild int8->:bigger = ralloc(v.items =>! wild int8->, nc * #size_of<T>());
    v.items = bigger =>! wild T->;
    v.cap = nc;
    pass NIL;
};

// ---- `Pod`: what `vec_get` may hand back by value (S-23a; RX-168) -----------
//
// ONE METHOD, AND THE LANGUAGE DECIDES WHO MAY IMPLEMENT IT. `pod_copy` takes
// its `self` LENT and returns a `Self`, never failing. For a type that owns
// nothing, `pass self` is its copy; for an owning type the same body is
// NITPICK-TYPE-047 -- a lent owner cannot be passed on -- so an impl the
// compiler admits for one would have to build an independent value. That is what
// makes `T: Pod` on `vec_get` S-23a's rule stated TO THE COMPILER, at every
// consumer's instantiation, and not only by `check_vec_elements_own_nothing` over
// `src/`. Measured at `c970483`: `impl:string:Pod`, `impl:Bytes:Pod`,
// `impl:SparseSet:Pod` and `impl:Vec<int64>:Pod`, each written `pass self`, are
// each NITPICK-TYPE-047.
//
// THE SCALARS ARE HERE -- the integers to 64 bits and `bool`, every scalar this
// library's specifications store in a `Vec`. A struct that owns nothing
// implements it where it is declared, in one line like these; `core.npk`
// re-exports the name so a consumer can. A wider scalar is one line here.
//
// THE HOLE, stated where a reader meets it: the compiler also accepts an impl
// that declares its `self` `move` where the trait lends it, and a call through
// the trait then hands the callee a value the caller still owns -- a double
// free (95). The workbench registry's O-N28; `tests/probe/probe18_impl_adds_move.npk` pins it with no
// library code. No impl here does it, and the harness's S-23a check still
// refuses an owning element anywhere in `src/`.
pub trait:Pod = {
    func:pod_copy = Self(Self:self) never fails;
};
impl:int8:Pod = { func:pod_copy = int8(int8:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:int16:Pod = { func:pod_copy = int16(int16:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:int32:Pod = { func:pod_copy = int32(int32:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:int64:Pod = { func:pod_copy = int64(int64:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:uint8:Pod = { func:pod_copy = uint8(uint8:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:uint16:Pod = { func:pod_copy = uint16(uint16:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:uint32:Pod = { func:pod_copy = uint32(uint32:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:uint64:Pod = { func:pod_copy = uint64(uint64:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:bool:Pod = { func:pod_copy = bool(bool:self) never fails { pass self; }; };

// ---- the checked accessor pair ---------------------------------------------
//
// EVERYTHING BELOW THIS LINE THAT TOUCHES `.items[` HAS ALREADY CHECKED.

// obligation: never fails requires i >= 0i64 && i < v.count
//
// RETURNS BY VALUE, which at the non-owning `T` this type is for (S-23a) is a
// plain read. **AT AN OWNING `T` IT IS A MOVE, AND NOTHING REFUSES IT.** `pass`
// moves a place implicitly, and D-264 checks this body once with `T` treated as
// owning, so `Vec<string>` compiles and the first `vec_get` TAKES the element:
// the slot is zeroed and marked vacant, `count` still counts it, and a second
// `vec_get` of the same index returns an empty string -- through a parameter
// that is not even a pointer (`tests/unit/vec_owning_get_moves_out.npk`,
// measured at `c3bdae2`). UNTIL THE THIRD CYCLE-0.0 AUDIT (BL-5) THIS COMMENT
// SAID SUCH A COPY IS "refused, TYPE-046" -- false at every pin this repository
// has taken, since TYPE-046 is the diagnostic that ASKS for `move` and `pass`
// supplies one. The compiler's own `List<T>` has no by-value get at all -- `l[i]`
// is a bounds-checked place -- and that is the shape a cycle lifting S-23a
// would take (RX-155).
//
// ITS `Vec` IS A LOAN, NOT A COPY (the compiler's D-065 and D-183: an ordinary
// parameter is one the callee may read and may not keep). Since cycle 0.0.4d
// `Vec` is move-only, and a loan still needs no `move` -- which is what lets a
// consumer read a sealed field, `vec_get(s.sparse, k)`, without taking the
// address D-313 counts as a write. The fourth cycle-0.0 audit expected
// move-only to change this signature; measured at `c3bdae2`, it does not
// (RX-162). Nothing here writes through `v`, which is the whole of what makes a
// loan safe while the compiler does not hold a callee to it.
//
// *(Dated 2026-09-26, cycle 0.0.4e, compiler `c970483` -- RX-168. THE FIRST
// PARAGRAPH IS HISTORY. The pin refuses this body as it was, at every `T`:
// `pass v.items[i]` passes a `T` PLACE out of a lent parameter, and since the
// compiler's DEF-104 a bare `T` owns for that question -- NITPICK-TYPE-047, "this
// parameter was lent, not given". The language's copy of a `T` place is
// `.clone()` under `Clone`, whose `clone` the prelude declares MAY FAIL, so in a
// `never fails` body it is a `Result<T>` (NITPICK-TYPE-007; NITPICK-TYPE-042
// under `raw`). So this asks for `T: Pod` and reads through `pod_copy`, and at an
// owning `T` it is NITPICK-TYPE-017 -- the move the first paragraph describes
// cannot be written any more. Its `Vec` is still a loan, read-only now
// (NITPICK-TYPE-085); a consumer still reads a sealed field by value,
// `vec_get(s.sparse, k)`.)*
pub func:vec_get<T: Pod> = T(Vec<T>:v, int64:i) never fails {
    if (i < 0i64)        { drop vec_oob(i); }
    if (i >= v.count)    { drop vec_oob(i); }
    pass raw v.items[i].pod_copy();
};

// obligation: never fails requires i >= 0i64 && i < v.count
//
// AT AN OWNING `T` THE ELEMENT IT OVERWRITES IS ORPHANED: a `wild T->` slot does
// not drop, and this writes over it (`tests/unit/vec_owning_set_orphans.npk`,
// 92 under the managed-half cap). Outside S-23a; stated here, as it already was
// at `vec_truncate`, because the third cycle-0.0 audit found it said nowhere.
pub func:vec_set<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:i, move T:x) never fails {
    if (i < 0i64)        { drop vec_oob(i); }
    if (i >= v.count)    { drop vec_oob(i); }
    v.items[i] = move(x);
    pass NIL;
};

// ---- adding and removing ---------------------------------------------------

// obligation: never fails ensures v.count == old(v.count) + 1i64
pub func:vec_push<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, move T:x) never fails {
    // DEAD `Vec` -- see the header. Without this, `ralloc(<dangling>, 0)`.
    if (v.cap <= 0i64) { drop vec_oob(v.cap - 1i64); }
    if (v.count >= v.cap) {
        int64:nc = v.cap * 2i64;              // traps on overflow (D-210)
        wild int8->:bigger = ralloc(v.items =>! wild int8->, nc * #size_of<T>());
        v.items = bigger =>! wild T->;
        v.cap = nc;
    }
    v.items[v.count] = move(x);
    v.count = v.count + 1i64;
    pass NIL;
};

// obligation: never fails requires v.count > 0i64
//
// Returns the removed element BY VALUE, and it is OWNERSHIP-CORRECT AT EVERY
// `T`: `count` is lowered FIRST, so the element leaves the container with its
// ownership and drops at the caller's binding
// (`tests/unit/vec_owning_pop_moves_out.npk`). *(This said "scalar `T` only,
// for `vec_get`'s reason" until the third cycle-0.0 audit's triage -- but
// `vec_get` leaves its slot counted and `vec_pop` does not, RX-155.)* An empty
// pop is the caller's error and traps rather than returning a value nobody
// chose (D-123: a default that carries meaning is a value nobody chose).
// *(Dated 2026-09-26, cycle 0.0.4e, compiler `c970483` -- RX-168. The move is
// SPELLED now. `pass v.items[v.count]` passes a `T` place out of what a pointer
// reaches, and at `c970483` that is NITPICK-TYPE-047 at every `T` (DEF-104;
// D-264's `T` owns). `move(...)` is the prelude's own `list_pop`, and it is what
// the implicit move did: diffed at `c3bdae2`, `vec_pop<string>` writes the same
// vacancy into the slot past `count`, plus one temporary and its flag.)*
pub func:vec_pop<T> = T(Vec<T>->:v) never fails {
    if (v.count <= 0i64) { drop vec_oob(v.count - 1i64); }
    v.count = v.count - 1i64;
    pass move(v.items[v.count]);
};

// obligation: never fails requires i >= 0i64 && i <= v.count
//              ensures v.count == old(v.count) + 1i64
//
// `i == v.count` IS LEGAL AND IS NOT A TYPO: inserting at the end is a push,
// and the half-open bound is what makes `vec_insert` total over the positions
// an element can occupy.
//
// OWNERSHIP-CORRECT AT EVERY `T`: the shift moves from the top, so every slot
// it writes is past `count` or already moved from, and no live element is
// overwritten (`tests/unit/vec_owning_insert_moves.npk`, which checks the order
// as well, RX-155).
pub func:vec_insert<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:i, move T:x) never fails {
    if (i < 0i64)        { drop vec_oob(i); }
    if (i > v.count)     { drop vec_oob(i); }
    // DEAD `Vec` -- see the header. Without this, `ralloc(<dangling>, 0)`.
    if (v.cap <= 0i64)   { drop vec_oob(v.cap - 1i64); }
    if (v.count >= v.cap) {
        int64:nc = v.cap * 2i64;
        wild int8->:bigger = ralloc(v.items =>! wild int8->, nc * #size_of<T>());
        v.items = bigger =>! wild T->;
        v.cap = nc;
    }
    int64:j = v.count;
    while (j > i) decreases j - i {
        v.items[j] = move(v.items[j - 1i64]);
        j = j - 1i64;
    }
    v.items[i] = move(x);
    v.count = v.count + 1i64;
    pass NIL;
};

// obligation: never fails requires i >= 0i64 && i < v.count
//              ensures v.count == old(v.count) - 1i64
//
// ORDER-PRESERVING, so it is O(count - i). `vec_swap_remove` is the O(1) one
// and the engines use that; this exists because the parser's group stack needs
// order and a library that offers only the fast one invites a hand-written
// shift at the call site, which is a `.items[` outside this file.
//
// AT AN OWNING `T` THE REMOVED ELEMENT IS ORPHANED -- the shift writes over it,
// or, at the last index, `count` simply stops counting it
// (`tests/unit/vec_owning_remove_orphans.npk`). Outside S-23a (RX-155).
pub func:vec_remove<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:i) never fails {
    if (i < 0i64)        { drop vec_oob(i); }
    if (i >= v.count)    { drop vec_oob(i); }
    int64:j = i;
    int64:last = v.count - 1i64;
    while (j < last) decreases last - j {
        v.items[j] = move(v.items[j + 1i64]);
        j = j + 1i64;
    }
    v.count = v.count - 1i64;
    pass NIL;
};

// obligation: never fails requires i >= 0i64 && i < v.count
//              ensures v.count == old(v.count) - 1i64
//
// O(1), and it DOES NOT PRESERVE ORDER. That is stated here rather than left to
// the name: a caller who wanted order and reached for the fast one gets a
// silently reordered container, which no test of the container itself catches.
//
// AND AT AN OWNING `T` THE REMOVED ELEMENT IS ORPHANED, which is the larger
// hazard and was the one this comment did not mention until the third cycle-0.0
// audit (`tests/unit/vec_owning_swap_remove_orphans.npk`). Outside S-23a.
pub func:vec_swap_remove<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:i) never fails {
    if (i < 0i64)        { drop vec_oob(i); }
    if (i >= v.count)    { drop vec_oob(i); }
    v.items[i] = move(v.items[v.count - 1i64]);
    v.count = v.count - 1i64;
    pass NIL;
};

// ---- bulk operations -------------------------------------------------------

// obligation: never fails ensures v.count == min(n, old(v.count))
//
// FOR AN OWNING `T` THIS DISCARDS WITHOUT DROPPING and the discarded elements
// leak, invisibly to `exit 0` (S-22) -- measured under the memory cap by
// `tests/unit/vec_owning_truncate_orphans.npk`, which is also the third
// cycle-0.0 audit's N-13: this was the half of `0.0.4.md`'s managed-half item
// that had no test. Outside S-23a; the caller of an owning `Vec` could not
// discharge it without `vec_get`'s move, which is why S-23a exists (RX-155).
pub func:vec_truncate<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:n) never fails {
    if (n < 0i64)        { drop vec_oob(n); }
    if (n < v.count) { v.count = n; }
    pass NIL;
};

// obligation: never fails ensures v.count == 0i64
//
// The same managed-body caveat as `vec_truncate`
// (`tests/unit/vec_owning_clear_orphans.npk`, N-13). Capacity is retained, which
// is the whole point: a `Cache` reused across searches clears rather than
// reallocating, and `SAFETY.md` S-6 says a search allocates nothing.
pub func:vec_clear<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v) never fails {
    v.count = 0i64;
    pass NIL;
};

// obligation: never fails ensures result.count == n && result.cap >= n
//
// A `Vec` of `n` ZERO BYTES, ready to be indexed. This is what a `SparseSet`'s
// backing arrays want at construction: `sparse` must be readable at any key
// before anything has been written there, and reading uninitialised storage is
// not something to rely on. `calloc` is one call to the allocator and the
// zeroing is the allocator's, not a loop here.
//
// **THIS REPLACES A GENERIC `vec_fill`, WHICH THE LANGUAGE DOES NOT PERMIT, AND
// THE DIAGNOSTIC IS WORTH KEEPING.** `vec_fill<T>(v, n, T:x)` writing `x` into
// `n` slots is refused `NITPICK-TYPE-046`:
//
//     `T` is a type parameter, and this body is checked once for every type it
//     is instantiated at -- some of which own storage (D-264): a copy here
//     would leave two owners at those.
//
// A generic body is checked against EVERY instantiation, not against the ones a
// caller happens to write, so an operation that copies its element cannot be
// generic at all — there is no `T` for which "write this value into n slots" is
// meaningful when `T` owns. `move` does not rescue it either: the value is
// consumed by the first slot. So the fill that is expressible is the one that
// needs no value, and that is this.
pub func:vec_init_zeroed<T> = Vec<T>(int64:n) never fails {
    // A NEGATIVE `n` WAS THE ONE WAY THIS LIBRARY COULD BUILD AN INVALID `Vec`
    // ITSELF, and it was found by the triage that fixed RX-139 rather than by
    // the audit that prompted it. Every other entry point in this file traps on
    // a negative (`vec_reserve` on `need`, `vec_truncate` on `n`, both
    // accessors on `i`); this one floored the BLOCK to one element and then
    // wrote `n` straight into `count`. `vec_init_zeroed(-5)` therefore returned
    // `count == -5, cap == 1`, `vec_push`'s `v.count >= v.cap` read `-5 >= 1`
    // and was FALSE, and the push wrote at `items[-5]` -- five elements BEFORE
    // the block, at exit 0, silently. Measured at `3d15ac9`.
    //
    // The floor below cannot serve as the guard, because the defect is in
    // `count` and the floor is on the allocation. `vec_init` is unaffected: it
    // sets `count: 0i64` whatever it was asked for.
    if (n < 0i64) { drop vec_oob(n); }
    int64:k = n;
    if (k < 1i64) { k = 1i64; }
    wild int8->:mem = calloc(k, #size_of<T>());
    pass Vec{ items: mem =>! wild T->, count: n, cap: k, move_only: [] };
};

// ---- the managed half ------------------------------------------------------

// FREES THE BLOCK **AND DROPS EVERY ELEMENT**, for an owning `T`.
//
// This is the function `exit 0` cannot check and a memory cap can. Dropping
// requires MOVING each element out — `move(v.items[j])` binds the element to a
// local that drops at the end of its iteration. It is separate from `vec_free`
// for COST: `vec_free` is O(1) at every `T` and this is O(`count`), and at the
// non-owning `T` `SAFETY.md` S-23a allows, both are correct -- so this exists
// for the managed-half gate and the owning units, which measure what S-23a
// excludes (RX-155). RX-126 records that this shape is outside the compiler's
// DEF-8 entirely: `Vec<T>` does not drop, so there is no drop flag to get
// wrong.
//
// *(Until the third cycle-0.0 audit's triage the reason given here was that "a
// `move` out of an element is refused for a `T` that does not own (there is
// nothing to move), so one function cannot serve both". False at `3d15ac9` and
// at `c3bdae2`: `vec_free_owning::<int64>` compiles and runs, because a `move`
// of a scalar is its copy (D-264).)*
//
// obligation: never fails ensures v.cap == 0i64
pub func:vec_free_owning<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v) never fails {
    // DEAD `Vec` -- see the header (RX-144). Worse here than in `vec_free`:
    // without it the loop below MOVES elements out of a freed block and drops
    // them, so a `vec_free_owning` after a `vec_free` runs destructors over
    // reclaimed memory before `dalloc` ever notices.
    if (v.cap <= 0i64) { drop vec_oob(v.cap - 1i64); }
    int64:j = 0i64;
    int64:live = v.count;
    while (j < live) decreases live - j {
        // Bound, then dropped at the end of this iteration's scope. Binding is
        // the point: an owning value that is never bound is never dropped,
        // which is the compiler's own D-183 debt.
        T:e = move(v.items[j]);
        drop drop_element(move(e));
        j = j + 1i64;
    }
    v.count = 0i64;
    dalloc(v.items =>! wild int8->);
    v.cap = 0i64;
    pass NIL;
};

// The sink an owning element is moved into so that it drops. It does nothing
// with the value on purpose: taking it by `move` makes this function the
// owner, and it drops at the closing brace like any other bound owner.
func:drop_element<T> = NIL(move T:e) never fails {
    discard(e);
    pass NIL;
};
